'''Asikyira''', officially the '''Asikyiran Republic''' ({{Langx|wo|Nguuru Asikiir}}) is a country located at the western tip of the continent of [[Abyala]], sharing land borders with [[Balisca]] to the north and [[Lacashe]] to the west. Asikyira is the ??-most populous country in the [[Western Hemisphere]] and the fourth-largest by total area. The capital of [[Sanu-Sasso]], while its most-populous city is [[Kuluba]].

Asikyira emerged as an independent state on 29 June 1743, when the colony of [[Bindorama]] — then under the suzerainty of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] — declared independence through the [[Bindorama Revolution]]. The young republic spent its first decades consolidating territorial control through the [[Pacification of Bindorama]] (1783–1826), a prolonged campaign to subdue indigenous resistance in the interior. The 19th century saw Asikyira engage in two major conflicts with its southern neighbour: the [[Lacashe-Asikyira War]] (1838–1840), fought over gold-bearing mountain territory, and the [[War of Liberation]] (1853–1856), in which Asikyira joined a coalition to abolish [[W:Chattel slavery|chattel slavery]] in Lacashe by force. The latter conflict led to the creation of [[Libertalia]], a [[W:Freedman|freedman]] state that would unite with Asikyira in 1889. The 20th century was dominated by the [[Simb Revolution]] of 1969, a left-wing revolution that overthrew the existing government and established a socialist-oriented state, and the subsequent transition to the current federal parliamentary republic under the [[Constitution of Asikyira|1996 constitution]].

Asikyira is a [[W:Federalism|federal]] [[W:Parliamentary republic|parliamentary republic]], the only such system among the major Abyalan states. The [[National Congress (Asikyira)|National Congress]] serves as the federal legislature. The country's economy is driven primarily by agricultural exports — including cotton, sugar, and tropical crops — along with fishing and copper mining. With a GDP (PPP) of I$1.021 trillion and an HDI of 0.818, Asikyira is classified as a developing economy with high human development. The population is predominantly [[Waajuri language|Waajuri]]-speaking, with significant [[Sikani language|Sikani]], [[Wulani language|Wulani]], and [[Classical Aruanãyára language|Aruanãyára]] linguistic communities. A majority of the population identifies as irreligious, a legacy of the secularization policies pursued during and after the Simb Revolution.

== Etymology ==

The name ''Asikyira'' derives from the [[Sikani language|Sikani]] word ''Asikyire'', meaning "[[W:Sugar|sugar]]" — a reference to the [[W:Sugar cane|sugar cane]] cultivation that defined the colonial economy of Bindorama and the coastal lowlands that constituted the core of the new republic. The adoption of a Sikani-derived name reflected the demographic and economic significance of the Sikani-speaking communities of the eastern lowlands, whose militancy in agricultural fields had been central to the independence movement. The Waajuri form, ''Nguuru Asikiir'', is a phonological adaptation of the same root, while the Wulani ''Asikyira Ka Bèjèfanga'' translates roughly as "Asikyira of the free people." 

The name was formally adopted at the [[Congress of Kuluba]] in 1745, two years after the Bindorama Revolution, replacing the colonial designation ''Bindorama'' — a Burawi corruption of the [[Kurupiran languages|Kurupiran]] ''Pindorama'', which means "the land of palm trees." The adoption of an indigenous-derived name for the new republic was a deliberate act of political symbolism, asserting the state's identity as distinct from its colonial origins and rooted in the agricultural landscape that had shaped its people.

== History ==

=== Pre-colonial Abyala ===

{{main|Aruanãyára Empire}}

Prior to Illypnian contact, the territory of present-day Asikyira lay at the southern periphery of the [[Aruanãyára Empire]], the largest political entity in pre-colonial Abyala. The Aruanãyára civilization, centered on the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] highlands to the northeast, had established a network of tributary provinces and trade routes extending to the Aethiopic coast by the 15th century. The southern provinces — corresponding roughly to the interior highlands of modern Asikyira — served as the empire's primary source of gold and precious stones, and the mountain passes connecting the highlands to the coast were controlled by Aruanãyára garrison towns whose ruins remain scattered across the [[Sikasso Plateau]].

The Aruanãyára settlements in the mountain valleys were among the most urbanised indigenous communities in pre-colonial Abyala, maintaining complex social hierarchies, [[W:Terrace farming|agricultural terracing]] systems, and long-distance trade networks. The coastal lowlands, primarily inhabited by the Tapuya and related semi-nomadic peoples who maintained seasonal agricultural circuits, fishing settlements, and trade contacts with the Aruanãyára highland communities. The relationship between the coastal and highland peoples was primarily commercial: the coastal communities served as intermediaries in the exchange of marine resources and tropical products for the gold and gemstones extracted from the interior. 

Neither the Tapuya nor the Aruanãyára exercised direct sovereignty over the entire extent of the territory that would become Bindorama, a circumstance that would later facilitate Burawi settlement.

=== Colonial Bindorama (1516–1743) ===
{{main|New Burawa|Burawi Kingdom of Galwa}}
Illypnian contact with the western Abyalan coast began in 1516, when the Burawi navigator [[Guled Badweyn]] established a fortified trading post at the site that would become [[Gulega]] — the future provincial capital of Bindorama. The settlement was the second Burawi foothold in Abyala, following the founding of [[Lacashe]] to the south in the same year. Together with the northern province of [[Arakwaya]], these three territories constituted [[New Burawa]] ({{langx|so|Burawi Cusub}}), the totality of Burawi colonial possessions on the eastern Abyalan seaboard.

Bindorama — known to the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisanian Crown]] as ''[[La Frontera]]'' ("the frontier") — occupied a coastal strip with a narrow plain that quickly gave way to the rising [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] range. The province was the smallest of the three but contained the most urbanised indigenous populations, who had established settlements in the mountain valleys prior to Burawi conquest. The Burawi conquest of the [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára highland empire]] in the 1570s, and the joint Burawi-Volisanian conquest of the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] in the 1560s, extended colonial control over the full extent of the eastern mountain range and secured the Bindorama interior.

Unlike Lacashe, where the [[W:Plantation economy|plantation economy]] relied on [[W:Chattel slavery|chattel slavery]], Bindorama's labour system was based primarily on [[W:Indentured servitude|indentured servitude]]. The province's mountainous terrain was less suited to the large-scale plantation agriculture that dominated the south, making it unattractive to the slave-owning elite and the central colonial authorities. This relative neglect by the colonial administration offered greater autonomy to the settler population, including more freedom to practice distinct cultural and religious traditions without interference from the Church authorities who held greater sway in Lacashe. The majority of colonial-era migrants to Bindorama came from the northern regions of the Burawi homeland, particularly from [[Dakare]] and surrounding areas. These Dakari settlers spoke [[Waajuri language|Waajuri]] and brought with them distinct cultural practices, agricultural techniques adapted to hill terrain, and kinship networks that shaped the province's settlement patterns. Many Dakari established smallholdings in the mountain valleys, engaging in mixed agriculture and small-scale mining rather than the plantation monoculture that dominated Lacashe. The crown offered land grants in Bindorama as an incentive for settlement in a province that Burawi authorities struggled to control effectively.

Alongside the Dakari, Bindorama received Sikani-speaking settlers from the central Burawi territories and Wulani-speaking migrants from the western savanna regions of the kingdom. These communities established themselves in the coastal lowlands and the foothills, where they cultivated sugar cane, tobacco, and cotton, and developed the agricultural cooperatives that would become a defining feature of the provincial economy. The Ayori peoples from the neighbouring mainland also contracted to work the land under indenture, typically for terms of ten years, after which they received their freedom and, in many cases, land grants.

Over time, the colonial population of Bindorama developed a distinctive character, shaped by the interaction of Dakari, Sikani, and Wulani cultural traditions with the residual Aruanãyára presence in the mountain valleys and the Burawi colonial administration. The Dakari-derived Waajuri dialect spoken in Bindorama absorbed influences from Aruanãyára languages and, to a lesser extent, from Volisanian traders along the mountain frontier. A mixed-ancestry class emerged — the ''Signares,'' a term derived from the Waajuri word for the female merchants who dominated the coastal trade — who accumulated significant economic power through their control of commerce and their role as intermediaries between the colonial administration and the settler population.

=== Internal tensions and slavery ===
{{Main|Slavery in the Empire of Burawa}}
The relationship between the colony and the metropole was strained from the outset. The [[Kingdom of Burawa]] exercised nominal sovereignty over New Burawa but provided limited military protection or administrative support, particularly in the frontier province of Bindorama. The late colonial period saw the gradual marginalisation of the [[Hoga aristocracy]] (and their [[Hoggaanka]]) and the consolidation of colonial governance under direct royal administration — a policy that affected Lacashe most directly, but that also shaped Bindorama, where the crown used land grants to channel displaced Hoga families and Dakari migrants into the province, simultaneously draining peninsular aristocratic power and strengthening the colonial frontier.

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]], which had sanctioned the enslavement of non-Christians since the early colonial period, underwent a significant doctrinal shift in 1695, when a synod of Burawi bishops issued a declaration condemning the [[Aethiopian slave trade|slave trade]] as incompatible with Christian ethics. The declaration did not immediately free enslaved persons, but it announced the Church's intention to phase out the institution and called upon the crown to cease granting licences for the trade. The declaration caused outrage among the merchant houses of [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]] — the Guleed, Warsame, and Ciise families that dominated the colonial trade — and alienated a commercial class that had previously been aligned with the crown. In Bindorama, where the economy was based on indentured servitude rather than chattel slavery, the Church's declaration was received more favourably, reinforcing the province's growing sense of moral distinction from the slave-dependent south.

The intellectual ferment of the late colonial period reached Bindorama through the circulation of ''[[On the Dignity of All Peoples]]'' (1700), a treatise by a monastic scholar that argued all humans possessed inherent dignity by virtue of their creation in the divine image, and that this dignity was violated by slavery, extreme social hierarchy, and the arbitrary exercise of power. The treatise circulated widely among the literate classes of the province — Church officials, Signare merchants, and the emerging Dakari intelligentsia — and became a touchstone for the political ideas that would animate the independence movement.

The [[Burawi Imperial Split]] of 1709, which divided the kingdom into the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]] and the western [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]], shattered the colonial framework. Bindorama was assigned to the Kingdom of Galwa as a protectorate, provoking immediate resistance from Burawi loyalists who rejected the authority of the Galwa court and allied with indigenous Aruanãyára peoples who had their own grievances against the colonial order. The resulting civil war — pitting the Galwa-aligned colonial administration against a coalition of Burawi loyalists, Dakari settlers, and Aruanãyára communities — persisted for three decades, rendering Galwa authority in the province increasingly nominal.

=== Bindorama Revolution and independence (1743) ===
{{main|Bindorama Revolution}}
The independence of Asikyira was the culmination of a civil war that had persisted since the [[Burawi Imperial Split of 1709]]. For three decades, the Kingdom of Galwa had exercised only tenuous authority over Bindorama, its power contested by Burawi loyalists, Dakari settler militias, and Aruanãyára communities that had never accepted the province's assignment to Galwa. The civil war had never been formally resolved; it had simply settled into a stalemate in which Galwa governors administered the coastal towns while the interior valleys and highlands operated beyond their effective control.

The decisive catalyst was the [[Restoration Revolution]] of 1741–1742 in the eastern Kingdom of Burawa. The military commander [[Gali Darg]], claiming Galwa backing, attempted to overthrow King Iacob V and restore the unity of the Burawi kingdoms under a Galwa claimant. The revolution failed — Darg was captured and executed — but in the process, Darg's agents assassinated Iacob V, and the eastern kingdom was plunged into a constitutional crisis. The Galwa court, which had denied any connection to Darg, was nevertheless compromised by the affair. The Kingdom of Galwa's credibility was shattered, both domestically and in its protectorates. In Bindorama, the Galwa governor — already isolated and dependent on a garrison that had not been paid in months — found his authority evaporating.

The revolution proper began on 29 June 1743, when the [[Kuluba garrison]] — commanded by the Dakari-born officer [[Mamadou Ndiaye]] — declared independence from the Kingdom of Galwa and arrested the provincial governor. The declaration was read from the balcony of the [[Signare Palace]] in Kuluba by [[Aminata Diallo]], the most prominent Signare of the city and the principal financier of the independence movement. The declaration invoked the principle of self-determination, the moral authority of the Church's anti-slavery tradition, and the philosophical arguments of ''[[On the Dignity of All Peoples]]'' — and it pointedly distinguished Bindorama's indenture-based economy from the chattel slavery of Lacashe, which remained a protectorate of the eastern Kingdom of Burawa. The extent to which this distinction reflected genuine abolitionist sentiment rather than political expediency remains debated.

The early republic was a fragile construction. The revolutionary coalition — which united the Signare merchant class, the Dakari military establishment, and the Sikani and Wulani agricultural elites — shared a common interest in independence but diverged sharply on questions of domestic policy, territorial organization, and the status of the Aruanãyára populations of the interior highlands. The [[Congress of Kuluba]] (1745) established the basic framework of the new state: a parliamentary republic with a bicameral legislature, an elected head of state, and a federal structure that granted significant autonomy to the coastal, highland, and savanna regions. The name ''Asikyira'' was formally adopted at this congress, replacing the colonial designation ''Bindorama.''

=== Pacification of Bindorama (1783–1826) ===
{{main|Pacification of Bindorama}}
The most protracted and violent chapter of Asikyira's early national history was the Pacification of Bindorama — a forty-three-year campaign of military conquest and political subjugation directed against the indigenous Aruanãyára and related peoples of the interior highlands. The Aruanãyára communities of the [[Sikasso Plateau]] and the eastern mountain valleys had been conquered by Burawi forces in the 1570s and incorporated into the colonial provincial system, but their effective subordination had always been tenuous. The mountain valleys had preserved a degree of autonomy unusual in the colonial context, with indigenous communities negotiating with colonial governors and maintaining elements of their pre-conquest social organisation. The independence of Asikyira, however, brought a new and more assertive sovereign to the highlands — one that regarded the interior as both a territorial right and an economic necessity, as the gold deposits of the [[Kweneng Mountains]] were essential to the young republic's fiscal stability.

The Pacification began in 1783, when the [[Asikyiran National Army]] launched its first major expedition into the Sikasso Plateau under the command of [[General Ousmane Sow]]. The campaign was conceived as a rapid military operation to establish garrisons at key passes and mining sites, but it quickly became a war of attrition against Aruanãyára guerrilla forces who exploited the mountainous terrain with devastating effectiveness. The Aruanãyára resistance was organized around a network of fortified hilltop settlements — the ''guaritas'' — that served as military strongpoints, political centres, and religious sanctuaries. Each guarita was governed by a ''tendaru'' (war-chief), and the resistance coordinated through a council of tendari that met annually at the sacred site of [[Kuarahý-Retã]].

The conflict passed through several phases. The initial offensive (1783–1791) established Asikyiran control over the western passes but failed to penetrate the interior plateau. The second phase (1791–1804) saw the Asikyiran army adopt a strategy of encirclement and siege, gradually cutting off the ''guaritas'' from their agricultural base and forcing surrenders through starvation. The most determined resistance came from the [[Kweneng Confederacy]], a coalition of eastern Aruanãyára tendari who held out until 1812, when the fall of the [[Guarita of Amambay]] — after a siege of fourteen months — broke the back of organized resistance. The final phase (1812–1826) consisted of mopping-up operations against scattered guerrilla bands and the forced relocation of Aruanãyára populations from the highlands to designated reserves in the eastern lowlands.

The Pacification remains the most contested episode in Asikyiran historiography. The official narrative — maintained through much of the 19th and early 20th centuries — portrayed the campaign as a civilizing mission that brought law, order, and economic development to a lawless interior. Revisionist scholarship, beginning in the 1960s, has characterized the Pacification as a war of conquest and cultural destruction that decimated the Aruanãyára population, destroyed the indigenous political order, and seized the gold-bearing territories that would become the foundation of Asikyira's mineral wealth. The debate remains unresolved in contemporary Asikyiran public discourse.

=== 19th century ===
==== Lacashe-Asikyira War (1838–1840) ====
{{main|Lacashe-Asikyira War}}
The [[Lacashe-Asikyira War]], known in Asikyira as the [[Mountain War]] ({{langx|wo|Ngiré Kër}}), was fought between Asikyira and its southern neighbour [[Lacashe]] from 1838 to 1840. Lacashe had been the oldest Burawi colonial possession, founded in 1516, and had served as the economic heart of New Burawa for nearly three centuries. In 1789, the eastern Kingdom of Burawa had passed the [[Abolition of Slavery Act]], prohibiting the institution throughout the kingdom and its possessions. The Lacashian colonial elite, whose plantation economy was entirely dependent on enslaved Kaftian labour, refused to accept emancipation and unilaterally declared independence from Burawa — terminating the protectorate arrangement that had governed relations since the [[Imperial Split of 1709]]. Burawa, having just abolished slavery, lacked both the capacity and the political will to reimpose its authority by force. Lacashe thus became an independent slave-holding state, maintaining the institution for decades thereafter.

The war between Asikyira and Lacashe arose from a territorial dispute over the [[Kweneng Mountains]], a gold-bearing region on the border between the two states whose ownership had been ambiguous since the colonial period. The discovery of new gold deposits in the disputed territory in 1836 intensified the dispute, and Lacashe — whose cotton-based economy was increasingly dependent on mineral revenue to offset declining agricultural returns — invaded in 1838, seeking to occupy the mountain territory by force.

The Asikyiran army, battle-hardened by the Pacification campaigns, mounted an effective defense. The mountainous terrain favoured the defender, and the Aruanãyára auxiliaries recruited from the recently pacified highlands proved invaluable as scouts and irregular fighters. The war lasted two years and ended with Lacashe occupying an additional approximately 5,000 km² of mountain territory — a territorial loss for Asikyira that was deeply resented in Kuluba and Sanu-Sasso. The conflict concluded through diplomatic intervention by [[Balisca]] and the [[Kingdom of Galwa]], which brokered a ceasefire and established a joint boundary commission. The territorial loss in the Kweneng Mountains remained a source of Asikyiran grievance and a factor in the later War of Liberation.

==== War of Liberation (1853–1856) ====
{{main|War of Liberation (1853–1856)}}
The [[War of Liberation]] was the defining military conflict of Asikyira's 19th-century history. In 1853, a coalition of powers — [[Burawa]], [[Volisania]], [[Balisca]], and Asikyira — formed to end chattel slavery in Lacashe by force. Lacashe had maintained its slave-based economy for 64 years since its independence from Burawa in 1789, and the institution had become more entrenched, not less, with each passing decade. The coalition proclaimed abolition as a war aim and initiated a naval blockade of Lacashian ports. Asikyira's participation in the coalition was driven by both strategic interest — the war offered an opportunity to recover the Kweneng Mountain territory lost in 1840 and to establish a friendly state on the Lacashian coast — and genuine abolitionist sentiment, which had deep roots in Asikyiran political culture. The Church's anti-slavery declaration of 1695 and the philosophical arguments of ''[[On the Dignity of All Peoples]]'' had shaped Asikyiran intellectual life for generations, and though Bindorama's colonial economy had been based on indentured servitude rather than chattel slavery, the moral imperative of abolition resonated powerfully with a population that retained vivid cultural memory of coerced labour.

The Asikyiran army invaded Lacashe from the north, advancing through the mountain passes that had been contested in the previous war. The campaign was conducted in coordination with Baliscan forces operating along the coast and internal uprisings of enslaved and freedman combatants organized through the [[Freedman Congress]]. The Asikyiran contribution was substantial: approximately 25,000 troops were deployed over the course of the three-year war, and the Asikyiran army bore the brunt of the fighting in the mountainous interior. The war ended with the decisive defeat of the Lacashian planter state and the signing of the [[Treaty of Guledga]] on 12 September 1856, which abolished slavery, partitioned Lacashian territory, and imposed reparations and occupation arrangements, including the [[Porto Vren Concession]].

===== Libertad-Lacashe War (1875–1880) =====
{{main|Libertad-Lacashe War}}
In 1875, Lacashe launched an invasion of Libertalia, initiating the [[Libertad-Lacashe War]]. The war lasted five years and was conducted primarily on Libertalian territory, causing extensive devastation. Asikyira, as Libertalia's principal ally and the state with the most direct military interest in the balance of power along the Lacashian border, deployed a substantial army that eventually pushed Lacashian forces back across the border. The war ended in 1880 with a new peace treaty reimposing the partition and guaranteeing Libertalia's independence. The conflict had been economically devastating for both Libertalia and Lacashe, but particularly for Libertalia, whose territory had been the primary battlefield — a reality that accelerated the movement toward unification with Asikyira.

==== Unification with Libertalia (1889) ====
{{main|Libertalia|Unification of Libertalia with Asikyira}}
[[File:Flag of Libertalia.svg|thumb|The flag of the [[Republic of Libertalia]]]]
The [[Treaty of Guledga]] had partitioned the eastern coastal strip of Lacashe to create [[Libertalia]], a state established for freed slaves and freedmen under the governance of the [[Freedman Congress]]. For three decades, Libertalia existed as an independent state — but its existence was precarious. 

In 1875, Lacashe invaded Libertalia, initiating the [[Libertad-Lacashe War]]. The war lasted five years and was conducted primarily on Libertalian territory, causing extensive devastation. Asikyira, as Libertalia's principal ally and the state with the most direct military interest in the balance of power along the Lacashian border, deployed a substantial army that eventually pushed Lacashian forces back across the border. The war ended in 1880 with a new peace treaty reaffirming the partition and guaranteeing Libertalia's independence. The conflict had been economically devastating for both Libertalia and Lacashe, but moreso for Libertalia, whose territory had been the primary battlefield, which strengthened the case for unification with Asikyira.

The unification of Libertalia with Asikyira, formally completed on 22 October 1889, reflected both the economic devastation of the war and the close political ties that had developed between the Freedman Congress and Asikyiran political movements. The merger was negotiated over several years, with the [[Treaty of Sanu-Sasso]] (1883) establishing the framework for integration and the [[Asikyiran Accords of Union|Accords of Union]] (1889) finalizing the terms. The former Libertalian territory was incorporated as an [[W:Autonomous region|autonomous region]] within the larger state, with guaranteed representation in the National Congress and preserved local governance structures. The unification nearly doubled Asikyira's coastal territory and added approximately 800,000 citizens — predominantly of [[West Kaftia|West]] and [[Central Kaftia|Central Kaftian]] descent, freed from chattel slavery in Lacashe — to the national population, further reinforcing the demographic weight of the coastal lowlands relative to the interior highlands.

=== 20th century ===

==== Baliscan Revolution and Abyalan Cold War ====
{{Further|Baliscan post-revolution exodus|Abyalan Cold War}}

==== 1938 coup d'état and military rule ====
{{Main|1938 Asikyiran coup d'état|Military dictatorship in Asikyira}}

==== Simb Revolution (1969) ====

{{main|Simb Revolution}}

The [[Simb Revolution]] of 3 July 1969 was the most radical political upheaval in Asikyira's modern history. The revolution was led by the [[Simb Movement]] ({{langx|wo|Dëkk bi Simb}}), a left-wing coalition of Asikyiran nationalists, trade unionists, and socialist intellectuals that had been organizing in the urban centres since the early 1960s. The movement took its name from the ''simb'' — the Waajuri ceremonial lion dance, a symbol of popular power and resistance that the movement appropriated as its emblem.

The revolution was precipitated by a decade of political stagnation and economic crisis. The [[National Democratic Party (Asikyira)|National Democratic Party]], which had governed Asikyira since the restoration of civilian rule in 1946, had become increasingly authoritarian and corrupt, concentrating power in the hands of the Kuluba commercial elite while neglecting the agricultural interior and the former Libertalian coastal territories. The global economic depression that followed the end of the [[Great Adonian War]] and continued through the 1960s exacerbated existing inequalities, and a series of strikes and mass protests in the spring of 1969 created the conditions for the Simb Movement's seizure of power.

On the morning of 3 July 1969, Simb Movement cadres, supported by sympathetic elements of the Asikyiran National Army, seized control of government buildings in Sanu-Sasso and Kuluba. The [[Revolutionary Council of the Simb]] declared the dissolution of the National Congress and the suspension of the 1946 constitution. The council's founding proclamation announced the creation of a "people's republic" dedicated to agricultural reform, workers' control of industry, and the elimination of the "parasitic commercial oligarchy" that had dominated Asikyiran politics since independence.

The Simb regime pursued a programme of radical transformation. Large agricultural estates were expropriated and collectivized. Foreign-owned enterprises were nationalized. The education system was overhauled to emphasize Waajuri language instruction and socialist ideology. The regime's most enduring reform was the secularization programme, which dismantled the religious institutions that had been closely associated with the old regime and promoted state atheism — a policy that contributed to the high rate of irreligion in contemporary Asikyira. The Simb period was also marked by political repression: the Revolutionary Council suppressed opposition parties, restricted press freedom, and imprisoned or exiled prominent figures associated with the old regime.

The Simb regime governed Asikyira for nearly three decades, though its radicalism diminished over time. By the late 1980s, the regime had begun a process of controlled liberalization, permitting limited political pluralism and relaxing restrictions on private enterprise. The transition culminated in the adoption of the current constitution on 1 January 1996, which established the federal parliamentary republic that governs Asikyira today.

==== Kuluba Accords and regional diplomacy ====

{{main|Kuluba Accords}}

The [[Kuluba Accords]], signed in August 1982, formally ended hostilities between [[Balisca]] and [[Potocsí]] and committed both governments to non-interference in each other's internal affairs. The accords, negotiated in Kuluba under Asikyiran mediation, marked Asikyira's emergence as a significant diplomatic actor in the Abyalan region. The successful mediation enhanced Asikyira's international reputation and established the precedent of Asikyiran neutrality in regional conflicts — a posture that has defined the country's foreign policy in the post-Simb era.

=== Modern era (1996–present) ===

The adoption of the [[Constitution of Asikyira|1996 constitution]] marked the formal end of the Simb revolutionary period and the establishment of the current federal parliamentary republic. The constitution restored multi-party democracy, guaranteed civil liberties, established an independent judiciary, and created the federal structure that divides Asikyira into its constituent states. The transition was managed by the [[Transitional Council for Democratic Renewal]], a body composed of Simb-era reformers, opposition figures, and civil society representatives that governed the country from 1993 to the first elections under the new constitution in 1996.

Contemporary Asikyira faces several persistent challenges. The agricultural sector, while still the backbone of the economy, remains vulnerable to commodity price fluctuations and the effects of the Adonian climate crisis. The former Libertalian territories, despite decades of integration, continue to exhibit lower per capita income and higher unemployment than the national average. The Aruanãyára communities of the interior highlands, marginalized since the Pacification, have increasingly organized to demand land restitution and cultural recognition. The country's foreign policy, anchored in the principle of non-alignment, has sought to balance its relationships with the major powers while advocating for the interests of the Abyalan coastal states in international forums.

== Geography ==
{{Main|Geography of Asikyira}}
Asikyira occupies the southeastern tip of the Abyalan continent, with a total area of approximately 674,228 km². The country is bounded by the [[Aethiopic Ocean]] to the east and south, [[Balisca]] to the north, [[Potocsí]] to the northwest, and [[Lacashe]] to the southwest. The [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] mountain range extends through portions of the interior, forming a natural boundary with Lacashe and Potocsí to the west and creating a dramatic transition from the coastal lowlands to the highland interior.

The country's topography divides into three broad zones. The coastal lowlands, a narrow strip of alluvial plain extending along the Aethiopic coast, contain the majority of the population and the most productive agricultural land. The central highlands — the [[Sikasso Plateau]] and the [[Kweneng Mountains]] — rise sharply from the coastal plain, reaching elevations of 2,000 to 3,500 metres. These highlands contain the gold deposits that have been central to Asikyira's economy since the colonial period, as well as the coal and copper reserves that allowed the process of [[Industrialization in Asikyira|Asikyiran industrialization]] to begin in the late 1800s. The northern savanna, a vast expanse of grassland and scattered woodland, extends from the highlands to the Potocsí border to the coastal border with [[Araucaria]] ([[Balisca]]) and supports extensive cattle pastoralism and dryland agriculture.

The [[Joliba River]], the country's principal waterway, rises in the Kweneng Mountains and flows westward through the coastal lowlands to the Aethiopic Ocean. The Joliba and its tributaries drain approximately 60% of the national territory and provide the freshwater supply for the major cities. The [[Guledga River]], which forms part of the southern border with Lacashe, drains the southwestern highlands.

=== Climate ===

The coastal lowlands experience a tropical climate with a pronounced wet season from November to March and a dry season from April to October. Average temperatures range from 24 °C to 32 °C throughout the year, with high humidity. The central highlands are significantly cooler, with temperatures averaging 5–10 °C lower than the coast and greater diurnal variation. The northern savanna experiences a semi-arid climate with less reliable rainfall and higher temperatures, particularly during the dry season when the [[Harmattan (wind)|Harmattan]] — a hot, dry wind blowing from the Kaftian interior — can reduce visibility and damage crops.

The [[Aurora Current]], a cold nutrient-rich stream flowing along the Abyalan coast, moderates temperatures in the coastal zone and supports one of the most productive fisheries in the Aethiopic Ocean. The current's influence diminishes rapidly with distance from the coast, however, and the interior highlands and savanna experience far greater temperature extremes.

== Government and politics ==

{{main|Politics of Asikyira}}

Asikyira is a [[W:Federalism|federal]] [[W:Parliamentary republic|parliamentary republic]] under the [[Constitution of Asikyira|1996 constitution]]. The federal structure divides the country into constituent states, each with its own legislature and executive, subordinate to the [[National Congress (Asikyira)|National Congress]] in [[Sanu-Sasso]]. The head of state is the [[President of Asikyira]], elected by the National Congress for a single five-year term. The head of government is the [[Prime Minister of Asikyira]], who must command the confidence of the lower house of the National Congress.

The National Congress is a [[W:Bicameral legislature|bicameral legislature]]. The upper house, the [[Council of States (Asikyira)|Council of States]], represents the constituent states and the autonomous region of former Libertalia, with each state delegating an equal number of representatives. The lower house, the [[People's Assembly (Asikyira)|People's Assembly]], is elected through a mixed electoral system combining single-member districts with [[W:Proportional representation|proportional representation]]. The People's Assembly holds the primary responsibility for legislation, budget approval, and government oversight.

Prior the the [[Simb Revolution]], the National Assembly as a rare [[W:Tricameralism|tricameral]] body, operating under a [[W:Consociationalism|consociationalist-system]] that sought to give each of the three major political blocs in the country a legislative voice: the [[Libertalia|Libertalians]] in the south, the [[Indigenous peoples in Asikyira|Indigenous peoples]] in the mountainous interior, and the predominately-[[Waajuri people|Waajuri]] coast, where the majority of the population resided. [[W:Legislative gridlock|Legislative gridlock]] and political dysfunction was a significant factor in the lead-up to the [[1938 Asikyiran coup d'état|military coup of 1938]] and subsequent military rule. 

=== Political parties ===

The contemporary Asikyiran political spectrum includes several major parties, reflecting the country's complex post-Simb political landscape:

* '''[[Asikyiran Socialist Party]]''' — the direct successor to the Simb Movement, advocating democratic socialism, agricultural cooperatives, and continued state involvement in the economy. The dominant party since the 1996 constitution.
* '''[[National Democratic Party (Asikyira)|National Democratic Party]]''' — a centrist party advocating market-oriented economic reform, private enterprise, and closer integration with international trade frameworks. The principal opposition party.
* '''[[Aruanãyára People's Alliance]]''' — an indigenous rights party representing the Aruanãyára communities of the interior highlands, advocating land restitution, cultural autonomy, and environmental protection.
* '''[[Libertalia Front]]''' — a regional party based in the former Libertalian territories, advocating increased autonomy and economic development for the coastal region.
* '''[[Waajuri National Congress]]''' — a conservative nationalist party emphasizing Waajuri cultural identity and traditional values.

=== Administrative divisions ===

Asikyira is divided into eight constituent states and one autonomous region. The autonomous region of [[Libertalia (autonomous region)|Libertalia]] — comprising the former Libertalian territory incorporated in 1889 — maintains its own regional assembly and exercises devolved powers over education, cultural affairs, and regional economic development. The constituent states each have their own legislature and executive, with powers defined by the federal constitution.

== Economy ==
{{Main|Economy of Asikyira}}
As of 1 May 2026, the [[List of countries by GDP (PPP)|gross domestic product (PPP)]] of Asikyira was I$1.021 trillion, measuring to GDP per capita (PPP) of I$28,642. As such, Asikyira is classified as an [[W:Industrialized economy|industrialized economy]] with high human development. The Gini coefficient of 32.6 indicates moderate inequality — significantly lower than that of neighbouring [[Lacashe]] and [[Potocsí]], a consequence of the [[W:Land redistribution|land redistribution]] and cooperative formation policies pursued during the Simb period. The economy retains significant state involvement, particularly in the mining and agricultural sectors, though the post-1996 liberalization has permitted the growth of private enterprise in manufacturing, services, and tourism.

Asikyira's diversified economy includes agriculture, industry and a wide range of services. The large service sector accounts for about 72.7% of total GDP, followed by the industrial sector (20.7%), while the [[Agriculture in Brazil|agriculture sector]] is by far the smallest, making up 6.6% of total GDP.<ref>{{Cite web|title=GDP – composition, by sector of origin|url=https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/field/gdp-composition-by-sector-of-origin/|access-date=3 October 2022|website=[[The World Factbook]]|publisher=[[Central Intelligence Agency]]|archive-date=19 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230419042310/https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/field/gdp-composition-by-sector-of-origin/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The agricultural sector is organized around a mixture of large-scale export plantations and smaller cooperative farms — the latter a legacy of the Simb-era collectivization, which was partially reversed under the [[1996 Constitution of Asikyira|1996 constitution]] but left intact the cooperative structures that had become entrenched in rural communities. Asikyira is one of the [[List of largest producing countries of agricultural commodities|largest producers of various agricultural commodities]], and also has a large [[cooperative]] sector that provides almost 70% of the food in the country. It has been the world's largest [[Sugar production in Asikyira|producer of sugar]] for the last 150 years and is one of the world's largest producers of sugarcane, soy, coffee, maize, cotton, lemon, tobacco, pineapple, banana, beans, coconut, watermelon and papaya; and is one of the top 10 world producers of cocoa, cashew, mango, rice, tomato, sorghum, tangerine, avocado, persimmon, and guava, among others.

[[W:Subsistence agriculture|Subsistence agriculture]] — primarily rice, [[W:Millet|millet]], and [[W:Cassava|cassava]] — remains widespread in the northern savanna and the interior highlands.

In the [[Mining in Asikyira|mining sector]], Brazil is among the largest producers of iron ore, copper, coal, bauxite, manganese, tin, [[niobium]], and nickel. In terms of precious stones, Asikyira is the world's largest producer of [[amethyst]], [[topaz]], [[agate]] and one of the main producers of [[tourmaline]], [[emerald]], [[Aquamarine (gemstone)|aquamarine]], [[garnet]] and [[opal]]. The country is a major exporter of soy, iron ore, pulp (cellulose), maize, beef, chicken meat, soybean meal, sugar, coffee, tobacco, cotton, orange juice, footwear, airplanes, cars, vehicle parts, gold, ethanol and semi-finished iron, among other products.

== Demographics ==

{{main|Demographics of Asikyira}}

The population of Asikyira is approximately 35,672,891 as of the 2026 estimate, making it the ?? most populous country in the Western Hemisphere. The population is concentrated in the coastal lowlands, which contain approximately 70% of the total population on roughly 30% of the national territory. The two largest cities — [[Kuluba]] (the economic capital) and [[Sanu-Sasso]] (the administrative capital) — together account for approximately 8 million residents.

=== Languages ===

The official language of Asikyira is [[Waajuri language|Waajuri]], spoken as a first language by approximately 65% of the population and as a second language by the majority of the remainder. [[Sikani language|Sikani]] and [[Wulani language|Wulani]] are recognized as national languages and are used in education, media, and regional administration in the areas where their speakers are concentrated. [[Classical Aruanãyára language|Aruanãyára]] is spoken by the indigenous communities of the interior highlands and is recognized as a national language under the 1996 constitution, though its use in formal contexts remains limited.

=== Religion ===

A majority of the Asikyiran population — 55.2% as of the 2023 census — identifies as irreligious. This is a direct legacy of the Simb Revolution's secularization programme, which dismantled religious institutions, promoted state atheism, and integrated secularism into the educational system. The remaining 44.8% of the population includes adherents of [[Burawi Catholicism]], [[Ẹnímímọ́]], [[Candomblé]] (a Burawi-Ayori [[W:Syncretic religion|syncretic religion]]), and various indigenous spiritual traditions. The [[Candomblé]] community, concentrated in the former Libertalian coastal territories, has influenced and maintained significant connections to the broader Kaftian spiritual revival.

== Culture ==

{{main|Culture of Asikyira}}

Asikyiran culture is a product of the interaction between Kaftian-descended majority traditions, indigenous Aruanãyára heritage, and the residual influences of the Burawi colonial period. The Waajuri oral tradition — particularly the ''griot'' ({{langx|wo|géwél}}) storytelling tradition — remains a vital cultural force, with professional storytellers preserving and performing historical narratives, genealogies, and moral tales that predate the colonial period. The ''Simb'' lion dance, from which the Simb Revolution took its name, is performed at festivals and ceremonies throughout the country and has become a symbol of national identity.

Music is central to Asikyiran cultural life. The coastal regions are known for their polyrhythmic drumming traditions, which draw on Waajuri, Sikani, and Wulani musical heritage and have influenced the development of popular music across the Abyalan continent. The former Libertalian territories have produced distinctive musical forms that blend Kaftian rhythmic structures with Mesogean harmonic traditions, creating genres that have found audiences across the Aethiopic world. The [[Kuluba]] music scene, in particular, has been a crucible for experimental fusions of traditional and electronic forms.

Asikyiran cuisine reflects the country's agricultural base and its Kaftian cultural roots. Rice and millet are the staple grains, typically served with fish stews (in the coastal regions) or meat and vegetable sauces (in the interior). The national dish, ''thieboudienne'' ({{langx|wo|ceebu jën}}, "rice and fish"), is a reflection of the coastal fishery's importance to the national diet. Tropical fruits, cashew nuts, and sugar-derived sweets are widely consumed, and the northern savanna regions maintain a distinctive pastoralist cuisine based on grilled meats and fermented dairy products.

